{"parentUuid":"0816b410-5fbf-4f3e-8e5f-dc6aae9f7e14","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a99a44401cf927d52","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01WyKRCRabMqpJHscccULU7S","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01CoQHxJyU5sjMNUxqMu5JvN","name":"WebFetch","input":{"url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2412.09444v1","prompt":"Summarize the GP2S paper: Search Strategy Generation for Branch and Bound Using Genetic Programming. What does it evolve, what's the function set, what problems it tested on, what are the results?"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1635,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4127,"cache_read_input_tokens":22151,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4127,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":53,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"}},"requestId":"req_011CaoxnqTQ7A8tm1xKTYXJr","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"2f46cc75-f6f0-4f71-8564-5cf9a4cf76af","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:02:08.601Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"d39c201e-bed9-4749-a73a-9fec301ff4dd","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"5ea2c3ce-bea8-4ce3-8195-f1f0f2991204","agentId":"a99a44401cf927d52","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0193mt1o4MHkmbEttbdcw2i5","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"genetic programming\" \"branch and bound\" search strategy 2024\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Search Strategy Generation for Branch and Bound Using Genetic Programming\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2412.09444v1\"},{\"title\":\"[2412.09444] Search Strategy Generation for Branch and Bound Using Genetic Programming\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.09444\"},{\"title\":\"Search Strategy Generation for Branch and Bound Using Genetic Programming | Proceedings of the AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence\",\"url\":\"https://ojs.aaai.org/index.php/AAAI/article/view/33229\"},{\"title\":\"Selecting the Best Lower-Bound Strategy in a Branch-and-Bound Algorithm Using Genetic Programming | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-032-15455-2_4\"},{\"title\":\"Search Strategy Generation for Branch and Bound Using Genetic Programming\",\"url\":\"https://ojs.aaai.org/index.php/AAAI/article/download/33229/35384\"},{\"title\":\"Search strategy generation for branch and bound using genetic programming | Proceedings of the Thirty-Ninth AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence and Thirty-Seventh Conference on Innovative Applications of Artificial Intelligence and Fifteenth Symposium on Educational Advances in Artificial Intelligence\",\"url\":\"https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1609/aaai.v39i11.33229\"},{\"title\":\"Learning to search in branch-and-bound algorithms\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/289581769_Learning_to_search_in_branch-and-bound_algorithms\"},{\"title\":\"Genetic Branch-and-Bound or Exact Genetic Algorithm? | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-540-79305-2_12\"},{\"title\":\"Genetic Programming Applied to Mixed Integer Programming | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-540-24650-3_11\"},{\"title\":\"simulation_outcomes/MIPLIB/time_limit_80_function_GP_seed_3_partition_test.json · main · UltraBO / Search Strategy Generation for Branch and Bound using Genetic Programming · GitLab\",\"url\":\"https://gitlab.univ-lille.fr/ultrabo/search-strategy-generation-for-branch-and-bound-using-genetic-programming/-/blob/main/simulation_outcomes/MIPLIB/time_limit_80_function_GP_seed_3_partition_test.json?ref_type=heads\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about genetic programming and branch-and-bound search strategy research in 2024:\n\n## Recent Development: GP2S\n\nA novel machine learning approach called GP2S (Genetic Programming for Search Strategy) automatically generates a Branch-and-Bound search strategy heuristic, aiming to make intelligent decisions while being computationally lightweight.\n\n## The Problem Being Addressed\n\nBranch-and-Bound (B&B) is an exact method in integer programming that recursively divides the search space into a tree, and during the resolution process, determining the next subproblem to explore within the tree—known as the search strategy—is crucial. Hand-crafted heuristics are commonly used, but none are effective over all problem classes, and recent approaches utilizing neural networks claim to make more intelligent decisions but are computationally expensive.\n\n## How GP2S Works\n\nThe policy space is explored using a genetic programming algorithm, and the policy that achieves the best performance on a training set is selected.\n\n## Performance Results\n\nThe method is at most 2 percent slower than the best baseline and consistently outperforms SCIP, achieving an average speedup of 11.3 percent. Additionally, GP2S exceeds SCIP's average performance in 7 out of 10 cases across 15 times more instances.\n\nThis work represents an emerging area where genetic programming—previously little-explored in combination with integer linear programming—is being applied to improve branch-and-bound algorithms' efficiency.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"68ecf008-3cc4-463d-a601-ab6c64e9189b","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:02:12.501Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"d39c201e-bed9-4749-a73a-9fec301ff4dd","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"0816b410-5fbf-4f3e-8e5f-dc6aae9f7e14","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"5ea2c3ce-bea8-4ce3-8195-f1f0f2991204","agentId":"a99a44401cf927d52","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015cbjG7ABcTvkDNWTnjz6e1","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"DEAP pyshgp PushGP python library 2025\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"PyshGP: PushGP in Python\",\"url\":\"https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3067695.3082468\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - erp12/pyshgp: Push Genetic Programming in Python.\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/erp12/PyshGP\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) PyshGP: PushGP in Python\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/318377320_PyshGP_PushGP_in_Python\"},{\"title\":\"pyshgp · PyPI\",\"url\":\"https://pypi.org/project/pyshgp/\"},{\"title\":\"PyshGP: PushGP in Python\",\"url\":\"http://gpbib.cs.ucl.ac.uk/gp-html/Pantridge_2017_GECCO.html\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - erp12/pyshgp-demo: Demo usage for https://github.com/erp12/pyshgp\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/erp12/pyshgp-demo\"},{\"title\":\"PyshGP: PushGP in Python Edward Pantridge MassMutual Financial Group\",\"url\":\"https://dl.acm.org/doi/pdf/10.1145/3067695.3082468\"},{\"title\":\"PyshGP 0.1.8 documentation - ERP\",\"url\":\"https://erp12.github.io/pyshgp/\"},{\"title\":\"pyshgp 0.1.8 on PyPI - Libraries.io - security & maintenance data for open source software\",\"url\":\"https://libraries.io/pypi/pyshgp\"},{\"title\":\"PyshGP | Proceedings of the Genetic and Evolutionary Computation Conference Companion\",\"url\":\"https://dl.acm.org/doi/abs/10.1145/3067695.3082468\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's information about DEAP, PyshGP, and PushGP Python libraries:\n\n## PyshGP - Push Genetic Programming in Python\n\nPyshGP is Push Genetic Programming in Python. It is an open-source PushGP framework implemented in the Python programming language, with features for data science applications.\n\n### Purpose and Features\n\nPyshGP aims to bring PushGP to a wider range of users and use cases, making it easier to compare PushGP with other methods or build ML pipelines that contain PushGP and other models together. Although PushGP is constantly changing through research and publication, pyshgp is meant to be a slowly changing, more stable, PushGP framework.\n\n### About PushGP\n\nPushGP is a leading software synthesis system that utilizes stochastic (typically evolutionary) search methods to produce programs capable of manipulating all the common data types, control structures, and data structures. PushGP has discovered novel quantum computer programs previously unknown to human programmers, and has achieved human competitive results in finding algebraic terms in the study of finite algebras.\n\n### Selection Methods\n\nTree selection methods are supported by pyshgp, with the default selection method being Lexicase Selection, while Epsilon-Lexicase Selection and Tournament Selection are two other currently implemented selection methods.\n\n**Note:** The search results did not contain specific information about DEAP or PyshGP updates specific to 2025. The latest version found was 0.1.8.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"f9e4217c-11ed-4a72-9f75-cff572f1ec71","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:02:13.620Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"0816b410-5fbf-4f3e-8e5f-dc6aae9f7e14","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"2f46cc75-f6f0-4f71-8564-5cf9a4cf76af","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"5ea2c3ce-bea8-4ce3-8195-f1f0f2991204","agentId":"a99a44401cf927d52","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CoQHxJyU5sjMNUxqMu5JvN","type":"tool_result","content":"# GP2S: Search Strategy Generation for Branch and Bound\n\n## Overview\nGP2S uses genetic programming to automatically generate search strategy heuristics for Branch-and-Bound (B&B) solvers in mixed-integer linear programming (MILP).\n\n## What Gets Evolved\nThe method evolves **node scoring functions** represented as tree structures. These functions rank B&B nodes to determine exploration order in a best-first search strategy.\n\n## Function Set\nThe GP uses:\n- **Operators**: Addition, subtraction, multiplication, and protected division\n- **Terminals**: Node features (depth, best estimate, lower bound) and model features (dual bound at root, constraint/variable counts, plus a large constant M)\n\n## Test Problems\nExperiments occurred in two phases:\n\n**Phase 1** (Synthetic problems):\n- Fixed Charge Multicommodity Network Flow (FCMCNF)\n- Maximum Satisfiability (MAXSAT)  \n- Generalized Independent Set Problem (GISP)\n\n**Phase 2** (Real-world):\n- MIPLIB 2017 dataset (758 instances tested)\n\n## Key Results\n- On synthetic problems: \"within 2% of the best baseline and achieves an average speedup of 11.3% over SCIP\"\n- On MIPLIB 2017: \"surpasses SCIP in 7 out of 10 cases\" when evaluated on substantially larger test sets with extended time limits\n- Methods are computationally lightweight compared to neural network baselines"}]},"uuid":"ea000f15-e4d3-4db0-96b1-a71eb5b41d3c","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:02:15.831Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"2f46cc75-f6f0-4f71-8564-5cf9a4cf76af","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
