{"parentUuid":"e8315f16-cb23-4372-a648-e3f8e04bddbb","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a99a44401cf927d52","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01MUhQFYmuUMziv1YjuwDXPo","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01PbxmvJmFGRcTXgSZJDVi2Q","name":"WebSearch","input":{"query":"symbolic regression discover cuts inequalities optimization 2024 2025"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5138,"cache_read_input_tokens":26278,"output_tokens":237,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5138},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":237,"cache_read_input_tokens":26278,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5138,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5138,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}},"requestId":"req_011CaoxoxKg9iVKkyZxvh8fD","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"26d08c72-dc3e-4d01-966a-9119c85c6cfe","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:02:20.079Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"272c59e2-bbe5-42fe-b578-9b6ef6c9e811","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"5ea2c3ce-bea8-4ce3-8195-f1f0f2991204","agentId":"a99a44401cf927d52","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Bm2uMvanvi6GPNby4B5SGY","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"genetic programming\" \"nogood\" learning constraint solver 2024 2025\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Nogood Learning for Mixed Integer Programming Tuomas Sandholm Rob Shields\",\"url\":\"https://www.cs.cmu.edu/~sandholm/nogoodsForMip.techReport06.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"GI@ICSE 2025: 14th International Workshop\",\"url\":\"https://geneticimprovementofsoftware.com/events/papers.html\"},{\"title\":\"CP: Principles and Practice of Constraint Programming 2027 2026 2025 ...\",\"url\":\"http://www.wikicfp.com/cfp/program?id=572\"},{\"title\":\"Genetic Programming: 27th European Conference, EuroGP 2024, Held as Part of EvoStar 2024, Aberystwyth, UK, April 3–5, 2024, Proceedings | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/978-3-031-56957-9\"},{\"title\":\"Exploring the Effect of Genetic Improvement for Large Language Models-Generated Code | SN Computer Science | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s42979-025-04281-x\"},{\"title\":\"Genetic algorithms for the solution of optimisation problems (CHEER v13 n1)\",\"url\":\"https://www.economicsnetwork.ac.uk/cheer/ch13_1/ch13_1p16.htm\"},{\"title\":\"Inter-instance Nogood Learning in Constraint Programming | Springer Nature Link (formerly SpringerLink)\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-642-33558-7_19\"},{\"title\":\"CP 2024\",\"url\":\"https://cp2024.a4cp.org/\"},{\"title\":\"GENOCOP: a genetic algorithm for numerical optimization problems with linear constraints: Communications of the ACM: Vol 39, No 12es\",\"url\":\"https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/272682.272711\"},{\"title\":\"A hybrid heuristic transfer framework for combinatorial optimization: dual-tree genetic programming and neural networks for bin packing and knapsack problems | Neural Computing and Applications | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00521-025-11790-6\"}]\n\nThe search results contain relevant information about genetic programming and constraint solving topics from 2024-2025, though the specific combination of all four terms together doesn't appear in a single recent publication.\n\nHere are the key findings:\n\n**Nogood Learning in Constraint Solving:**\nNogood learning is an effective CSP technique critical to success in leading SAT solvers, and has been extended for use in combinatorial optimization including mixed integer programming (MIP). Lazy Clause Generation is a powerful approach to reducing search in Constraint Programming, achieved by recording sets of domain restrictions that previously led to failure as new clausal propagators called nogoods.\n\n**Recent Genetic Programming Work (2024-2025):**\nThe EuroGP 2024 conference proceedings focused on Evolution to Approach Problems in the Synthesis, Improvement, and Repair of Computer Programs. \n\nCartesian Genetic Programming (CGP) is a form of Genetic Programming that has been successfully used in a large variety of tasks including data-driven symbolic regression, and has been formulated for use in Genetic Improvement problems.\n\nAdditionally, a novel hybrid heuristic transfer framework has been introduced that enables the adaptation and reuse of learned heuristics across different optimization problems.\n\nWhile the search results show active research in both genetic programming and constraint solving with nogoods, I did not find a specific 2024-2025 publication that explicitly combines all these terms together.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"96a37da9-bc04-4ac1-a27f-27f90d0bcde7","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:02:25.315Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"272c59e2-bbe5-42fe-b578-9b6ef6c9e811","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"e8315f16-cb23-4372-a648-e3f8e04bddbb","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"5ea2c3ce-bea8-4ce3-8195-f1f0f2991204","agentId":"a99a44401cf927d52","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01S56ribrEFuF3aN4Xrv8NPC","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"FunSearch AlphaEvolve vs genetic programming comparison combinatorial optimization 2025\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"2025-5-16 AlphaEvolve: A coding agent for scientific and algorithmic discovery\",\"url\":\"https://msu.dvaoblaka.ru/media/2025/05/68271bf34ef55_AlphaEvolve.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"CodeEvolve: an open-source evolutionary framework for algorithmic discovery and optimization\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2510.14150v3\"},{\"title\":\"Can We Use AI to Discover Better Algorithms? | Richard Cornelius Suwandi\",\"url\":\"https://richardcsuwandi.github.io/blog/2025/llm-algorithm-discovery/\"},{\"title\":\"Global Optimization for Combinatorial Geometry Problems ...\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2601.05943\"},{\"title\":\"CodeEvolve: An open source evolutionary coding agent for algorithm discovery and optimization\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2510.14150v1\"},{\"title\":\"Some comments on AlphaEvolve Ernest Davis Dept. of Computer Science\",\"url\":\"https://cs.nyu.edu/~davise/papers/AlphaEvolveNotes.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"AlphaEvolve: A Gemini-powered coding agent for designing advanced algorithms — Google DeepMind\",\"url\":\"https://deepmind.google/blog/alphaevolve-a-gemini-powered-coding-agent-for-designing-advanced-algorithms/\"},{\"title\":\"AlphaEvolve: A coding agent for scientific and algorithmic discovery\",\"url\":\"https://storage.googleapis.com/deepmind-media/DeepMind.com/Blog/alphaevolve-a-gemini-powered-coding-agent-for-designing-advanced-algorithms/AlphaEvolve.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Even with AI, Bijection Discovery is Still Hard: The\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.20987\"},{\"title\":\"[2506.13131] AlphaEvolve: A coding agent for scientific and algorithmic discovery\",\"url\":\"https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2506.13131\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's a comprehensive overview of the comparison between FunSearch, AlphaEvolve, and genetic programming in the context of combinatorial optimization in 2025:\n\n## FunSearch vs. AlphaEvolve\n\nFunSearch paired an LLM with a programmatic evaluator to discover novel solutions to open problems in mathematics, establishing the viability of the approach for scientific discovery. AlphaEvolve generalizes FunSearch from evolving single functions to entire codebases and a broader range of optimization tasks, including GPU kernels, warehouse-scale computing, and complexity theory.\n\nKey differences between the two systems include:\n\n- **Scope of Evolution**: Unlike FunSearch, which focuses on discovering single functions, AlphaEvolve is designed to evolve entire codebases and develop much more intricate algorithms.\n\n- **LLM Architecture**: Unlike FunSearch that uses a single LLM, AlphaEvolve uses an ensemble approach combining Gemini Flash and Gemini Pro. The lightweight Gemini Flash enables higher rates of candidate generation through lower latency, while the more powerful Gemini Pro provides deeper insights and higher-quality suggestions.\n\n- **Sample Efficiency**: FunSearch generated millions of samples in its search for effective code; AlphaEvolve needs only thousands.\n\n## Performance on Combinatorial Optimization\n\nAlphaEvolve was applied to over 50 open problems in mathematical analysis, geometry, combinatorics and number theory. In roughly 75% of cases, it rediscovered state-of-the-art solutions, and in 20% of cases, AlphaEvolve improved the previously best known solutions.\n\n## Comparison with Genetic Programming\n\nAutomated generation and optimization of computer programs has long been the domain of Genetic Programming, where populations of programs are iteratively improved by operators such as crossover and mutation. The modern LLM-driven approaches like AlphaEvolve represent an evolution of traditional genetic programming, incorporating neural language models to guide the search process more effectively than classical genetic algorithms alone.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"0a76b160-c040-42c5-884a-72ddd511dc99","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:02:27.000Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"e8315f16-cb23-4372-a648-e3f8e04bddbb","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"26d08c72-dc3e-4d01-966a-9119c85c6cfe","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"5ea2c3ce-bea8-4ce3-8195-f1f0f2991204","agentId":"a99a44401cf927d52","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PbxmvJmFGRcTXgSZJDVi2Q","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"symbolic regression discover cuts inequalities optimization 2024 2025\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Recent Advances in Symbolic Regression | ACM Computing Surveys\",\"url\":\"https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3735634\"},{\"title\":\"AI Feynman: A physics-inspired method for symbolic regression | Science Advances\",\"url\":\"https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/sciadv.aay2631\"},{\"title\":\"Interpretable scientific discovery with symbolic regression: a review | Artificial Intelligence Review | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10462-023-10622-0\"},{\"title\":\"Rethinking Symbolic Regression Datasets and Benchmarks for Scientific Discovery\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2206.10540v5\"},{\"title\":\"A physics-guided symbolic regression framework for efficient and interpretable sand constitutive modeling\",\"url\":\"https://cdnsciencepub.com/doi/10.1139/cgj-2025-0201\"},{\"title\":\"Call for Action: Towards the Next Generation of Symbolic Regression Benchmark\",\"url\":\"https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/20250004553/downloads/SRBench_GECCO_2025_workshop_v3.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Discovering network dynamics with neural symbolic regression | Nature Computational Science\",\"url\":\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s43588-025-00893-8\"},{\"title\":\"Symbolic Regression and Equation Discovery Workshop | HEAL\",\"url\":\"https://heal.heuristiclab.com/research/symbolic-regression-workshop\"},{\"title\":\"Symbolic regression via MDLformer-guided\",\"url\":\"https://proceedings.iclr.cc/paper_files/paper/2025/file/a402493de088886740b5939f666a6e56-Paper-Conference.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Interactive symbolic regression with co-design mechanism through offline reinforcement learning | Nature Communications\",\"url\":\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-025-59288-y\"}]\n\nI found several relevant results on symbolic regression, cuts, and optimization from 2024-2025. Here are the key findings:\n\n## Recent Advances in Cuts and Optimization for Symbolic Regression\n\nKim et al. introduced new cuts for mixed-integer nonlinear programs (MINLPs) and developed a heuristic for iteratively constructing expression trees. This work is directly relevant to your search query, as it addresses how cutting planes can improve MINLP-based symbolic regression approaches.\n\n## Mixed-Integer Programming Approaches\n\nIn symbolic regression, mixed-integer programming (MIP) is a commonly used deterministic method, but its computational efficiency is low. To address this, Neumann et al. enhanced MINLP efficiency by representing equations as directed acyclic graphs, reducing binary variables.\n\n## Recent 2024-2025 Research\n\nSeveral recent studies are pushing symbolic regression forward:\n\n- A neural symbolic regression approach autonomously uncovers network dynamics from data and was demonstrated to refine existing models of gene regulation and ecology, and identify epidemic transmission patterns across spatial scales. (Published October 2025)\n\n- The Symbolic Q-network is an advanced interactive framework for large-scale symbolic regression that leverages reinforcement learning without relying on a transformer-based decoder, and proposes a co-design mechanism where the framework facilitates effective interaction with domain experts at any stage of the equation discovery process. (Published April 2025)\n\n- SR4MDL offers a range of capabilities, making it a powerful tool for uncovering symbolic laws that underlie diverse complex systems. (Published 2025)\n\nThese recent advances show significant progress in combining cutting-plane techniques, optimization methods, and machine learning for improved symbolic regression.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"083c989d-8ea1-4cad-b238-1130110fd0b2","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:02:27.266Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"26d08c72-dc3e-4d01-966a-9119c85c6cfe","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
